   "thinking": "The issue is now clear. The `ledger-queue-0` pod is pending because:\n\n1. It's a StatefulSet pod with a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) `data-ledger-queue-0`\n2. The PVC is backed by a local PersistentVolume `local-pv-ip-10-0-7-21-nvme1` that has node affinity set to `ip-10-0-7-21.ec2.internal` (the failed node)\n3. The scheduler can't place the pod on any other node because:\n   - 1 node (ip-10-0-7-21) has the unreachable taint\n   - 8 other nodes conflict with the volume's node affinity, since local volumes are permanently bound to the node they're physically attached to\n\nThis is the fundamental problem with local NVMe storage: the data lives only on that failed host's drive, so if the node is truly gone, that data is likely gone or stranded with it.\n\nI'm worried about the reclaim policy being `Delete` \u2014 with Redis AOF data sitting on the local NVMe and the infra team still unsure if the host recovers, deleting the PV/PVC to reschedule onto a new node would mean losing all buffered settlement data, so this really is a genuine risk of permanent data loss.\n\nThis needs to be escalated: either we wait for the failed node to come back (safest for data integrity) or we accept starting fresh with an empty Redis and lose unacknowledged settlement messages. The scheduler can't move the pod elsewhere anyway since the volume's node affinity ties it to the dead host.\n\nLet me look into local persistent volume documentation to better understand the constraints and have solid grounds to cite in the escalation.\n\n",
